{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Patchset 2 pushed to change 2367 (commit cc1b386f3a88, Change-Id preserved: I80dac5f829df7c3d4fa8e453d885a8d094badcba). CI run 33231276142.\n\nPatchset 2 changes NO code — only claims. Patchset 1 over-stated the fix in four places, and each is now corrected to match the log evidence above:\n\n- Commit subject/body: 'stops fetching a remote manifest' -> 'stops resolving it remotely', plus an explicit paragraph naming the residual `getArtifact` fetch, its file:line, and follow-up bug 5caa-4b63-7ea2-4e71.\n- CHANGELOG: headline changed from 'CI no longer depends on a network fetch to raw.githubusercontent.com before every job' (false) to 'uv is pinned to an exact version, so CI can no longer silently change which uv it runs' (true), with a scope paragraph stating that one fetch per job remains.\n- pyproject.toml comment: the sentence calling this 'the very fetch this pin exists to remove' now distinguishes the resolution fetch (removed) from the artifact-lookup fetch (not removed).\n- docs/local-dev-env.md: 'The pin also removes a CI failure mode' -> 'makes CI's uv deterministic', with a paragraph saying the `##[error]fetch failed` mode is still reachable.\n\nLocal gates on patchset 2: `make lint` PASS (guard included, zizmor no findings), `pytest tests/unit/test_uv_pin_guard.py` 11 passed. Patchset 1 earned `Verified +1` (run 33230196661, conclusion success) on functionally identical code; that vote was outdated by the push and CI is re-running.\n\nTicket deliberately left `in_progress`, NOT closed: acceptance criterion 2 is not met and only the operator should decide whether this lands as the partial fix."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787973844077551001,"uuid":"6c4cdaea-e8ef-4e29-8a53-d1af3a4e7386"}